docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:3:It begins with a simple disgust: the sense that too much of modern software arrives already owned. Not merely by license, but by assumption. By framework. By institution. By ambient pressure. You are told you are free to build, but the freedom is pre-shaped. The roads are laid down in advance. The vocabulary is chosen for you. The architecture is implied before you write the first line.
docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:29:It is an attempt to recover software as something direct, inspectable, and human-scale. A place where code is not translated into some other shape before it can live. A place where JavaScript is allowed to remain JavaScript. No npm. No webpack. No Babel. No TypeScript. No dependency pile. No build step. Just ES modules, real files, a static server, a browser, and the nerve to believe that is enough.
docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:74:It rejects the idea that the builder must think in shapes handed down by larger powers.
docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:76:And beneath all of that, it defends something older and simpler: when you build a thing, the thing should still feel like yours. Not because no one else can see it, but because it still bears the shape of your judgment. Because it has not been passed through an institutional filter before it was allowed to live. Because it has not been turned into ceremony. Because the files still sit there, intact, honest, waiting for anyone curious enough to open them.
